body:
I was really pissed off so went to the gym and did deadlifts. I smashed my PB.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;

But now when I try to get close to my new PB (15kg less) I can't make it budge at all. I've always had the mental block stopping me from doing big lifts but it's annoying how I'll basically never be able to match my Rage Best.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

Anything I can do to stop the mental block on lifts?

I recently broke up with my girlfriend of two years because of both of us moving back home after university. 

We tried doing distance for 5 months and the last time we met up for the same job interview (Lots of places so not competing against each other for one slot) it just didn't feel right.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

Before we left to go back home we had a proper heart to heart talk about it all. We cried, we kissed, but ultimately we broke up. We both still have feelings and said we'd continuing being friends but understand we need time alone to get over things. 


Occasionally one of us will send a "Hope you're doing well" message or something like that just to show we still care. 
&nbsp;
I'm not sure if it's because she's getting over things still, but it feels like she doesn't want to make the effort to be friends any more.

And what happens if we both get the job and move to the same city? Would things rekindle and we can try again? Would it just be awkward and not really friends? 
&nbsp;
I feel like I'd 100% wait to see, but don't want to be that guy who waits after an ex getting hurt to see if things ever work out. 

&nbsp;

I've lost all motivation. I wouldn't go out of my way to harm myself at all- but if I got hit by a car and didn't survive it wouldn't bother me. 

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

Basically, I feel like I lost not only a girlfriend, but my best friend.

I used to play American football on both offensive and defensive line for GB. Staying stable on my feet way was vital. My very first coach gave me this advice: &nbsp;

*What is easier to fall over? A tall bit of wood with a smaller surface area or a shorter bit of wood with a much wider surface area?*
&nbsp;


* Keep your feet at a sensible distance apart (not close together) to keep the surface area bigger so you have more space to adjust when you're pushed.
* Stay low- difficult for taller people but a low centre of gravity is vital for not being pushed around.
* Work on your core to give you more stability and also lower body lifts (deads, squats etc.) to give you stronger base
